.TH ESDM\-ES\-PKCS11 8 "2026-10-19" "ESDM @VERSION@" "ESDM Manual"
.SH NAME
esdm\-es\-pkcs11 \- serve the random number generator of a PKCS#11 token to the ESDM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B esdm\-es\-pkcs11
.RB [ \-\-module
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-token_label
.IR LABEL ]
.RB [ \-\-pin_file
.IR PATH " | "
.B \-\-pin_credential
.IR NAME ]
.RB [ \-\-socket
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-client_uid
.IR USER ]...
.RB [ \-\-entropy_rate
.IR BITS ]
.RB [ \-v ]
.br
.B esdm\-es\-pkcs11
.RB [ \-\-module
.IR PATH ]
.RB [ \-\-token_label
.IR LABEL ]
.B \-\-listen
.I [HOST]:PORT
.RB [ \-\-cert
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-key
.IR FILE ]
.RB [ \-\-ca
.IR FILE ]
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B esdm\-es\-pkcs11
is the PKCS#11 entropy source of the ESDM: it reads the random number
generator of a PKCS#11 token \- a smartcard, a USB token, an HSM, or SoftHSM
\- with
.B C_GenerateRandom
through libp11, and serves it as an external entropy source on a UNIX domain
socket, which the ESDM reads through one of its provider socket sources
.RB ( "esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket" ,
see
.BR esdm\-server (8)
and
.BR libesdm_es_ext (3)).
The ESDM itself needs nothing that knows of PKCS#11: the PKCS#11 module \-
code of the token vendor \- runs in this process, with the privileges the
token needs rather than with those of the ESDM, and the PIN is a secret of
this process alone.
.PP
The daemon loads the PKCS#11 module and takes the first slot holding an
initialized token with a random number generator \- with
.BR \-\-token_label ,
the first one whose token carries that label. It logs in as the user
(CKU_USER) only if a PIN is configured and the token asks for a login; most
tokens hand out random numbers without one.
.PP
Every failed login uses up one of the PIN retries of a token, after which the
token locks its PIN. A PIN the token refuses is therefore wiped from memory
and never tried again \- neither when the token is looked for again nor when
the module is loaded anew \- until the daemon is started again with another
PIN; the token is used without a login meanwhile, as far as it allows. Nor is
a PIN tried on a token that reports its user PIN locked. The status tells
either.
.PP
A token that is not there \- not inserted yet, pulled, or lost to a failed
.B C_GenerateRandom
\- is looked for again on the next request of the ESDM, at most every 5
seconds. When the slots of the module hold no usable token, the module is
loaded anew, as some modules \- SoftHSM among them \- only see a token added
after they were loaded once they are loaded again. A module that cannot be
loaded at all is tried again the same way: the daemon serves in any case, and
the ESDM credits it with nothing while it has no token.
.PP
Whenever a token is bound, it is tested: two reads that have to differ. A
token that repeats its output is credited with nothing until a later test
passes \- one of the self tests the ESDM asks for, or the one of the token
bound anew. While no token is bound, requests for data fail and
the ESDM credits the source with nothing; a self test passes, as there is
nothing to test.
.PP
With
.B \-\-listen
or sockets handed over by socket activation, the daemon serves the token to
the ESDM of other hosts instead, on SCTP with DTLS and certificates, through
their
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-relay (8)
\- see
.BR NETWORK .
.PP
The daemon serves until it receives
.BR SIGTERM ,
.B SIGINT
or \- on a UNIX domain socket \-
.BR SIGHUP ,
then logs out of the token, wipes the PIN, removes the socket and exits with
status 0. On the network,
.B SIGHUP
reloads the TLS files. A configuration it cannot run with \- no module, an invalid option,
a PIN that cannot be read \- ends it at start up with exit status 1.
.SH THE PIN
The PIN is never given on the command line, where every user of the machine
reads it, nor at build time. It is the first line of a file:
.IP \(bu 2
the file of
.BR \-\-pin_file ,
or
.IP \(bu 2
a systemd credential, read from
.IR $CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY :
the one named with
.BR \-\-pin_credential ,
or else
.B pkcs11\-pin
if the daemon has it \- see
.B LoadCredential=
and
.B LoadCredentialEncrypted=
in
.BR systemd.exec (5).
.PP
The line break ending the line is not part of the PIN. A file that is empty,
holds more than one line or more than 256 bytes, or is no regular file is
refused, and a PIN file open to every user is warned about.
Without either, no PIN is used. The daemon keeps itself out of core dumps and
out of the reach of
.BR ptrace (2)
of other processes of its user, as its memory holds the PIN.
.SH NAME AND ENTROPY RATE
The daemon declares the name of the source and its entropy rate to the ESDM
(INFO request of the protocol, see
.BR libesdm_es_ext (3)):
.TP
name
.B PKCS#11
followed by the token label of
.B \-\-token_label
if one is given and both make a valid name of up to 32 bytes \-
.B "PKCS#11 my\-token"
\- else
.BR PKCS#11 ,
or the name of
.BR \-\-name .
The ESDM shows the source under this name in its status and its log, with its
instance name (ExternalSocket, ExternalSocket1, ...) next to it, which tells
several daemons apart.
.TP
entropy rate
the one of
.BR \-\-entropy_rate ,
by default the one of the build (meson option
.BR es_pkcs11_entropy_rate ,
128 bits per 256 bits of data unless configured otherwise). The ESDM credits
the source at this rate unless a rate is configured for the source in the ESDM
.RB ( "esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket_entropy_rate" ),
which takes precedence.
.SH STATUS
The daemon answers the STATUS request of the ESDM, which shows the answer as
the
.B provider
member of the source in its own status, with the fields of the provider server
of
.BR libesdm_es_ext (3)
\- see
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-serve (8)
\- in
.B server
and those of the PKCS#11 source in
.BR plugin :
.TP
.B module
the PKCS#11 module, shortened to its file name if the path is too long for the
status;
.TP
.B token_label
the token label of
.BR \-\-token_label ,
null if none is configured;
.TP
.B token
the label of the token in use, null while there is none;
.TP
.B slot
the PKCS#11 slot ID of the token in use, null while there is none;
.TP
.B token_present
whether a token is in use;
.TP
.B logged_in
whether the daemon is logged in to it;
.TP
.B pin
.B none
without a PIN,
.B set
with one,
.B refused
once the token refused it,
.B locked
if the token reported its user PIN locked \- the PIN is wiped then;
.TP
.B repetition_test
the verdict of the last test of the output of the token:
.BR passed ,
.B failed
or
.B none
before the first one;
.TP
.B last_error
why no token is in use, or why the last request failed \- null once a token
is in use and delivers.
.PP
A token label and the module path go into the status as UTF-8 without control
characters, whatever bytes they hold: a byte that is not, and a control
character, are shown as a '?' each.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-module " \fIPATH\fR"
The PKCS#11 module to load, e.g.
.I /usr/lib/softhsm/libsofthsm2.so
or
.IR /usr/lib/opensc\-pkcs11.so .
By default the one of the build (meson option
.BR es_pkcs11_module_path );
required if the build names none.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-token_label " \fILABEL\fR"
Use the token of the label
.IR LABEL .
By default the one of the build (meson option
.BR es_pkcs11_token_label ),
or else the first token with a random number generator.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pin_file " \fIPATH\fR"
Read the user PIN from the first line of the file
.IR PATH ,
see
.BR "THE PIN" .
.TP
.BI \-\-pin_credential " NAME"
Read the user PIN from the systemd credential
.IR NAME ,
which has to be there. Without this option, the credential
.B pkcs11\-pin
is read if the daemon has it.
.B \-\-pin_file
takes precedence.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-socket " \fIPATH\fR"
The socket to serve on, by default
.IR /run/esdm\-es\-pkcs11/esdm\-es\-pkcs11.socket .
The directory of the default is created by the unit (see
.BR SYSTEMD );
a daemon started otherwise needs it to exist, or a socket of its own.
A socket left at the path by an earlier instance is replaced, any other file is
not.
.TP
.BR \-m ", " \-\-mode " \fIMODE\fR"
Access mode of the socket, octal. The default is 0666: the ESDM connects with
its privileges dropped, and the daemon serves only the clients of
.BR \-\-client_uid .
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-client_uid " \fIUSER\fR"
Serve clients running as
.I USER
\- a uid or a user name \- next to root. May be given up to 8 times; the
default is
.BR nobody ,
the user the ESDM runs as unless told otherwise
.RB ( "esdm\-server \-\-username" ).
Any other client is disconnected at once, so that it can neither take the
client slots the ESDM needs nor drain the token.
.TP
.BI \-\-entropy_rate " BITS"
Declare
.I BITS
of entropy per 256 bits of data, 0 to 256, see
.BR "NAME AND ENTROPY RATE" .
.TP
.BI \-\-name " NAME"
Declare
.I NAME
as the name of the source: 1 to 32 bytes of UTF-8 without control characters.
.TP
.B \-\-no_dtls
Do not offer DTLS to the clients. A build with DTLS (meson option
.BR dtls )
offers it by default, see
.BR libesdm_es_ext (3).
.TP
.BI \-\-listen " [HOST]:PORT"
Serve on the network, see
.BR NETWORK .
The further options of the network \-
.BR \-\-cert ,
.BR \-\-key ,
.BR \-\-ca ,
.BR \-\-crl ,
.BR \-\-crl_check_all ,
.BR \-\-crl_allow_expired ,
.BR \-\-reload_interval ,
.BR \-\-allow ,
.BR \-\-max_connections ,
.BR \-\-max_handshakes ,
.BR \-\-max_handshakes_per_source ,
.BR \-\-max_per_client ,
.BR \-\-handshake_timeout ,
.BR \-\-idle_timeout ,
.B \-\-username
and
.B \-\-groupname
\- are those of
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-serve (8).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Verbose logging; repeat for more. On the network, warnings and errors are
logged without it, as by the other daemons on the network.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version.
.SH NETWORK
A token on one host serves the ESDM of others: with
.BR \-\-listen ,
the daemon serves it on SCTP, with DTLS 1.2 and certificates on both sides and
SCTP-AUTH, exactly as
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-serve (8)
serves a plugin \- see
.B NETWORK
there, for the transport, the bounds of the connections, the rotation of the
TLS files and the CRLs. The ESDM of the other host reads it through its
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-relay (8).
.BR \-\-socket ,
.BR \-\-mode ,
.B \-\-client_uid
and
.B \-\-no_dtls
are refused on the network; the certificate of a client tells who it is. The
STATUS tells
.B sctp
as the
.B transport
of the server, and the SHA-256 of the client's certificate as its
.BR peer .
.PP
The shipped unit serves the local ESDM, without a network
.RB ( PrivateNetwork=yes ).
To serve on the network instead, override its command line and grant it the
network in a drop-in, e.g.
.IR /etc/systemd/system/esdm\-es\-pkcs11.service.d/net.conf :
.PP
.nf
[Service]
ExecStart=
ExecStart=/usr/bin/esdm\-es\-pkcs11 \-\-listen :@ES_EXT_NET_PORT@ $ESDM_ES_PKCS11_OPTIONS
PrivateNetwork=no
IPAddressDeny=
RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_INET AF_INET6
LoadCredential=cert:/etc/esdm/es\-ext\-net/server.crt
LoadCredential=key:/etc/esdm/es\-ext\-net/server.key
LoadCredential=ca:/etc/esdm/es\-ext\-net/ca.crt
.fi
.PP
and drop
.B \-\-es_ext_socket
of the daemon from the local ESDM. The daemon runs as
.B esdm\-es\-pkcs11
there already, and drops no privileges.
.SH THE ESDM
The ESDM reads the daemon through one of its provider socket sources: the
build has
.B es_ext_socket_num
of them (meson option, 1 by default), each configured with the n\-th
occurrence of the options below. A build with another provider \- an
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-serve (8)
\- next to the PKCS#11 daemon needs one more, as does a second token served by
a second daemon.
.TP
.BI "esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket " PATH
the socket of the daemon;
.TP
.BI "esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket_uid " USER
the user the daemon runs as: the ESDM accepts a provider running as root or as
this user only, and trusts it with the entropy rate it declares;
.TP
.BI "esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket_entropy_rate " BITS
optionally, a rate that takes precedence over the one the daemon declares.
.PP
The ESDM connects to the daemon at its start and again whenever the connection
is lost, at most every 5 seconds \- by a seeding, or by its monitor: it need
not be started after the daemon, and a daemon restarted is read again on its
own. Started before the ESDM, the daemon is read already for the initial
seeding.
.SH SYSTEMD
The shipped
.B esdm\-es\-pkcs11.service
runs the daemon as the system user
.B esdm\-es\-pkcs11
\- created by its
.BR sysusers.d (5)
file \- on
.IR /run/esdm\-es\-pkcs11/esdm\-es\-pkcs11.socket ,
in a sandbox without network, devices or capabilities. A user of its own
rather than a
.B DynamicUser=
because the ESDM accepts the daemon by the user it runs as, which it resolves
when it starts. The module, the token label and further options go into
.BR ESDM_ES_PKCS11_OPTIONS ,
set in a drop\-in:
.PP
.nf
# /etc/systemd/system/esdm\-es\-pkcs11.service.d/token.conf
[Service]
Environment="ESDM_ES_PKCS11_OPTIONS=\-\-module /usr/lib/softhsm/libsofthsm2.so \-\-token_label my\-token"
.fi
.PP
The unit imports the credential
.B pkcs11\-pin
from the credential store
.RB ( ImportCredential= ,
systemd 254 and later) \- the file
.IR /etc/credstore/pkcs11\-pin ,
or one encrypted with
.B "systemd\-creds encrypt \-\-name=pkcs11\-pin"
in
.I /etc/credstore.encrypted
\- and runs without a PIN if there is none. With an older systemd, a
.B LoadCredential=pkcs11\-pin:/etc/esdm/pkcs11\-pin
in the drop\-in gives it.
.PP
The user needs access to the token: a smartcard or USB token is reached
through pcscd, whose polkit rules have to let the user use the reader
(actions
.B org.debian.pcsc\-lite.access_pcsc
and
.BR org.debian.pcsc\-lite.access_card );
a SoftHSM token is a directory of files the user has to be allowed to read and
write, e.g. with
.B SupplementaryGroups=
of the group owning it \- the default token directory
.I /var/lib/softhsm
is writable in the sandbox. A module reaching its device directly needs
.B PrivateDevices=no
and a
.BR DeviceAllow= ,
a network HSM
.B PrivateNetwork=no
and
.B "RestrictAddressFamilies=AF_UNIX AF_INET AF_INET6"
in the drop\-in.
.PP
The unit is ordered before
.BR esdm\-server.service :
started together, the ESDM finds the token ready for its initial seeding;
otherwise it does without the token until the daemon is there. The ESDM is
told about the daemon with
.B ESDM_SERVER_OPTIONS
of its unit:
.PP
.nf
# /etc/systemd/system/esdm\-server.service.d/pkcs11.conf
[Service]
Environment="ESDM_SERVER_OPTIONS=\-\-es_ext_socket /run/esdm\-es\-pkcs11/esdm\-es\-pkcs11.socket \-\-es_ext_socket_uid esdm\-es\-pkcs11"
.fi
.SH EXAMPLE
A SoftHSM token, the PIN in a file only root can read, the daemon started by
root on a socket of its own \- the ESDM accepts a provider running as root,
while the unit runs it as the user
.B esdm\-es\-pkcs11
in the directory it creates, see
.BR SYSTEMD :
.PP
.nf
esdm\-es\-pkcs11 \-\-module /usr/lib/softhsm/libsofthsm2.so \\
	\-\-token_label my\-token \-\-pin_file /etc/esdm/pkcs11\-pin \\
	\-\-socket /run/esdm\-es\-pkcs11.socket
esdm\-server \-\-es_ext_socket /run/esdm\-es\-pkcs11.socket
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR esdm\-server (8),
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-serve (8),
.BR esdm\-es\-ext\-relay (8),
.BR libesdm_es_ext (3),
.BR systemd.exec (5),
.BR systemd\-creds (1)
.SH AUTHOR
Markus Theil <theil.markus@gmail.com>
